# selector/: runtime rules

## Speed (CPU is charged)
- Python stdlib only. No numpy, no coverage, no pytest, no threads.
  `IMPORT_CHECK` defaults to on (`IMPORT_CHECK=0` keeps the all-tests fallback). It may run one `python -S`
  subprocess, cwd `/tmp`, to fingerprint a patched copy of the package. Its CPU is charged.
  `EXPENSIVE_CHECK=1` may run a second `python -S` on that same copy to re-optimize the TPC fixture
  queries the changed files actually call. A failed import selects every test. Over 5 s of check CPU,
  the wider selection stands.
- Load nothing you don't need: the map is split per source file (`maps/lines/<path>.json`);
  load only the files a patch touches. Keep `maps/index.json` (file_union, opens, testfile, durations) small.
- Parse each original source at most once per run; cache ASTs in a dict.
- Print with `json.dumps(obj, separators=(",", ":"))`. Flush once at the end.
- Measure: at exit write `resource.getrusage(SELF)+getrusage(CHILDREN)` user+sys to stderr.

## Pipeline (one function per module)
patch.py → classify.py → rules.py → pack.py → main.py

## Selection rules, in this order. The union across all files of a patch is selected.
1. `sqlglot/**/*.py` with `ast.dump(old) == ast.dump(new)` (comment/whitespace only) → nothing.
   `EQUIV_ENUM` defaults to on. A statement whose only AST difference is `is`↔`==` or `is not`↔`!=`
   on an attribute of an Enum that does not override equality (`maps/enums.json`) is the same.
   `is None`↔`== None` counts too, only when the other name is annotated or assigned in that
   function as `str`, `int`, `bool`, `Token`, or `TokenType`. Anything else, including an
   `Expression`, stays a real change. `IntEnum` and `str` mixins stay real changes for `is`↔`==`
   against a member.
   `EQUIV_DEAD` defaults to on (`EQUIV_DEAD=0` keeps the line). Every changed line is in an
   if/elif/else/except/loop body that no test runs (no line-map entry, not an import line), and
   the condition line is untouched → nothing for that file. A body that still has a covered line stays.
2. Changed old lines: '-' lines by number; pure '+' insertions → the lines on both sides.
   `NEIGHBOR_OFF=1` maps a pure insertion to the statement it is inserted into.
   Map each line to the FIRST line of its innermost enclosing `ast.stmt` (coverage records
   multi-line statements by their first line). A `def`/`class`/decorator line → the whole body's lines.
3. Line covered by tests → those tests.
4. Line executed at import (`import_lines`): `file_union[file]`, plus
   - class-body edit of class C (not inside a function) → tests that instantiated C or a subclass
     (`maps/inst.json`, key `module:QualName`, MRO already expanded), unioned with `file_union`;
     if C is in `maps/import_classes.json` → every test (unless `IMPORT_CHECK` narrows it, below).
     `NARROW_CLASS` defaults to on. With it on, a class that is not created at import and that has an inst entry
     drops `file_union` and selects those tests plus tests whose source or an opened fixture
     contains a string literal the edit removed or added (`maps/words.json`, case-insensitive).
     `WORD_NO_EXPENSIVE=1` drops tests over 3s from that word match.
     `CLASS_CAP=1`, after the import check has narrowed, uses the class file's `file_union`
     when the class was instantiated by more than 400 tests.
     `DIALECT_FILE_OFF=1` does not add `tests/dialects/test_<name>.py` on top of line coverage
     of `sqlglot/dialects/<name>.py`.
     No inst entry, or a literal that is not a word, keeps `file_union`.
   - a changed line inside a function that also runs at import → every test.
     `IMPORT_CHECK=1` first imports a patched copy. A failed import, or a fingerprint
     that changes outside the edited class, still selects every test. A fingerprint
     that is unchanged, or that changes only on keys of the edited class, keeps the
     line rules and adds the class-usage tests of the changed keys.
     `EXPENSIVE_CHECK=1` then drops a selected test over 3 s that coverage added and no changed
     fixture, source read, or test file also names. The TPC executor tests drop when the patch
     stays out of `sqlglot/executor/` and `optimize().sql()` matches the pinned map on the queries
     that call a changed file. `test_max_nodes` stays for `parser.py`, `tokens.py`,
     `tokenizer_core.py`, `expressions/core.py`, or a line in `maps/max_nodes_lines.json`.
     `test_generator_memory` stays for `generator.py` or `expressions/core.py`.
   - module-level code (not in a class) keeps `file_union`; if tests also ran it → every test
5. Line never executed → nothing.
6. Docstring changed → doctest-running tests, if any exist (look in tests/ for doctest usage).
7. `tests/fixtures/**` or other data file → `opens[file]` (tests that opened it).
8. `tests/**/*.py` → every test in that file; `tests/helpers.py`, any conftest → full suite.
9. Anything else (pyproject, setup, sqlglotc, rust, unknown) → full suite.
- Deleted/renamed/new files: new `.py` under sqlglot/ that nothing imports → nothing; otherwise full suite.

## Packing (pack.py)
- For k in 1..8: LPT (longest first onto the least-loaded worker), cost = max load + 2000 ms·k.
- If ≤ 12 tests, search every assignment to those k workers, pruning a partial placement that cannot beat the best cost. Durations: `round(seconds*1000)` ms.
- If more than 12, after LPT one pass swaps a test on the busiest worker with a test on each other worker when that lowers the max load.
- Empty selection → `"workers": []`.

## Never
- Never call the network, never write outside /tmp, never read /data/answers.
- Never let an exception kill the run: wrap each commit; on any error select the full suite
  for that commit and log the error to stderr.
